<h2>The old regime and the new, side by side</h2>
<p>Before 1 October 2018, two provisions defined the landscape. Section 10 made specific performance available in limited situations, broadly where damages were inadequate or could not be ascertained, and Section 20 declared the jurisdiction discretionary, instructing courts that they were not bound to grant the relief merely because it was lawful to do so. The 2018 amendment substituted Section 10 and deleted Section 20 altogether.</p>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old">
    <h4>Before 1 October 2018</h4>
    <p>Specific performance was an exceptional, discretionary remedy. The plaintiff had to show damages were inadequate, and even then the court could refuse on broad equitable grounds under the old Section 20 — hardship, conduct, the bargain's fairness.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="arrow">→</div>
  <div class="col new">
    <h4>After 1 October 2018</h4>
    <p>The substituted Section 10 provides that specific performance shall be enforced by the court, subject only to Sections 11(2), 14 and 16. The old Section 20 discretion is gone; refusal must now be traced to a statutory ground.</p>
  </div>
</div>
<p>The word that carries the reform is "shall". Where the statutory conditions are met and no listed exception applies, the court is directed to enforce the contract. Courts have recognised that the amendment marks a deliberate legislative shift towards making specific performance the ordinary remedy for breach, in line with the policy of improving contract enforcement.</p>

<h2>The surviving gateways: Sections 11(2), 14 and 16</h2>
<p>The new regime is a rule with defined exits, not an unconditional command. Section 11(2) preserves the bar on enforcing a contract made by a trustee in excess of the trustee's powers or in breach of trust. Section 16 preserves the plaintiff-side disqualifications, of which the most litigated remains Section 16(c): the plaintiff must aver and prove readiness and willingness to perform the essential terms of the contract. That requirement is untouched by the amendment, and suits continue to fail on it.</p>
<p>The recast Section 14 supplies the contract-side exceptions. A contract cannot be specifically enforced where the aggrieved party has obtained substituted performance of the contract; where performance involves a continuous duty which the court cannot supervise; where the contract is so dependent on the personal qualifications of the parties that the court cannot enforce specific performance of its material terms; and where the contract is in its nature determinable. Substituted performance — the aggrieved party's option to have the contract performed by a third party at the defaulter's cost — has its own detailed article on this site and is noted here only as the first of the four exceptions.</p>
<p>The determinable-contract exception deserves a drafting note. A contract that either party may terminate, with or without cause, is routinely argued to be determinable and hence outside specific enforcement. Termination clauses should therefore be drafted, and attacked, with this exception squarely in mind.</p>

<h2>The supporting machinery added in 2018</h2>
<p>The amendment did not stop at the substantive rule; it built procedure around it.</p>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h4>Section 14A — experts</h4><p>The court may engage one or more experts to assist it on any specific issue in the suit, direct parties to give the expert access to relevant information, and treat the expert's report as part of the record. Useful in technically dense enforcement disputes.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>Section 20A — infrastructure</h4><p>In suits relating to infrastructure project contracts, the court shall not grant an injunction where it would cause impediment or delay in the progress or completion of the project. Public-works defendants invoke this provision early and often.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>Section 20B — special courts</h4><p>State governments, in consultation with the Chief Justice of the High Court, may designate civil courts as special courts to try suits in respect of infrastructure project contracts.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>Section 20C — twelve months</h4><p>Suits under the Act are to be disposed of within twelve months from service of summons, extendable by six months for recorded reasons — a timeline plaintiffs should cite when pressing for expedition.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>What the shift means for plaintiffs</h2>
<p>Pleading priorities have changed. Under the old law, plaints laboured to show that damages were inadequate; that burden has largely fallen away for contracts outside Section 14. The energy now belongs elsewhere: a meticulous averment of readiness and willingness under Section 16(c), supported by a documented trail of performance capacity (funds arranged, obligations tendered, notices issued); and anticipatory pleading against the Section 14 exceptions the defendant is likely to raise, particularly determinability. A plaintiff in an infrastructure-adjacent dispute should also frame interim relief with Section 20A in view. Damages under Section 21 remain available in addition to or in substitution for specific performance, and the prudent plaint still claims them in the alternative, with the statutory requirement of pleading such compensation kept in mind.</p>

<h2>What the shift means for defendants</h2>
<p>Defences grounded in generalised hardship or in the adequacy of damages have lost their statutory home with the deletion of the old Section 20. A defendant resisting specific performance after 2018 must work within the surviving gateways: attack the plaintiff's readiness and willingness under Section 16; bring the contract within a Section 14 category, most commonly determinability or continuous supervision; or establish that substituted performance has already been obtained, which extinguishes the specific remedy. Conduct-based and equity-based arguments have not vanished from the courtroom, but they must now be anchored to a statutory provision rather than floated as free-standing appeals to discretion.</p>

<h2>The larger picture</h2>
<p>The 2018 amendment aligned Indian law with the position in many civil-law systems, where performance, not damages, is the primary response to breach. For transactional lawyers, the consequence is that contractual promises in India are more literally enforceable than they were: exit routes must be negotiated into the document, through termination rights and liquidated-damages structures, rather than assumed from judicial discretion. For litigators, the consequence is a remedy that is stronger but more technical — won or lost on Sections 11(2), 14 and 16, and on the quality of the record built before the suit was ever filed.</p>
